// campaigns domain -- email marketing campaigns as ordinary graph state
// (memql#3323).
//
// The whole point of modelling a campaign as CONCEPTS rather than as a
// bespoke table plus a bespoke API is that everything downstream then comes
// for free: the rows are browsable in the portal's concept browser, they are
// composable into views through the shared element library, they carry
// history like every other row, and they are authorization-gated by the same
// per-row machinery as the rest of the graph. A campaign editor is then a
// layout over queries and mutations, not a new subsystem.
//
// THIRTEEN CONCEPTS. Six answer the five questions a send has to answer --
// who, what, from whom, when, and what happened -- four are the sending
// engine's own state (memql#3348), and three are the compliance, engagement
// and automation surfaces layered on top:
//
//   audience         WHO        a named set of addresses
//   recipient        WHO        one address in an audience, with its subscription state
//   template         WHAT       the subject + bodies, authored once and reused
//   senderIdentity   FROM       a mailbox this deployment may send as (memql#4821)
//   campaign         WHEN       audience x template x schedule, plus the send state
//   delivery         OUTCOME    one row per recipient per campaign
//   sendJob          ENGINE     the per-campaign send run the worker drains
//   suppression      ENGINE     the cluster-wide do-not-mail list, digest-keyed
//   reputationWindow ENGINE     per (identity, domain, day) deliverability counters
//   warmupState      ENGINE     the evidence-driven ramp's own decision record
//   consentEvent     EXPORT     append-only grant/withdraw/bounce/complaint/suppress
//   engagementEvent  EXPORT     append-only open/click, one row per tracked hit (memql#4823)
//   emailRule        AUTOMATION "when X happens on concept Y, send template Z" (memql#4829)
//
// The census used to say EIGHT and list nine, having been written before the
// reputation and warmup rows landed. A count in a header is a claim the file
// makes about itself; keep it true when you add to this file.
//
// PER-ROW AUTHZ TIER: COMPOSITE, on the nine operator-facing concepts --
// @rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner) (memql#4820, program P2). Every
// row carries ownerUserId, every mutation stamps that field from actor.userId
// rather than from a caller argument, and every query names the composite
// predicate `(ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true)` as a
// top-level conjunct.
//
// WHY THE SECOND TERM, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY. These rows became a
// multi-client product surface: one deployment runs campaigns for several
// accounts, and the person who has to answer "why did this client's send
// stop" is the cluster owner. A plain owner tier has no cluster-owner escape
// on the READ path -- enforceRowAuthzOnPlan ANDs the owned predicate
// unconditionally -- so operator oversight would not merely be unimplemented,
// it would not be EXPRESSIBLE, and every fleet-wide campaigns view would
// silently render one person's subset.
//
// The second argument is READ-ONLY by construction: the WRITE guard ignores it
// (memql#4312), so direct row writes stay owner-scoped exactly as before. A
// cluster owner can therefore SEE another operator's campaign and drive its
// builtins -- whose gate is "can the caller read the campaign row" -- and
// cannot rewrite its content behind the owner's back. Full team sharing (a
// granted tier over a membership concept) stays a deliberate non-goal; this is
// oversight, not sharing.
//
// THE TWO ENGINE CONCEPTS ARE clusterOwner, NOT owned, and the split is the
// load-bearing part of the sending design (memql#3348).
//
//   `sendJob` is the queue the worker scans. A worker cannot scan an OWNED
//   concept at all -- the owned tier injects `ownerUserId==actor.userId` into
//   every read and has no cluster-owner bypass (rowauthz_enforce.go), so
//   "find every campaign that wants sending" is not a question an owned row
//   can answer. It is also not a question that SHOULD be answerable by a
//   caller-writable row: whichever row says "send campaign X as user Y" is,
//   by construction, an instruction the engine will carry out under Y's
//   authority. So the job row is clusterOwner-tier and every write to it is
//   internal-origin Go that derived campaignOwnerUserId from a row it had
//   already read UNDER THE CALLER'S OWN ACTOR. The caller proves ownership by
//   being able to read their own campaign; nothing they send is trusted.
//
//   `suppression` is the do-not-mail list, and it OUTRANKS EVERY AUDIENCE.
//   That phrase only means something if the list is one list: an unsubscribe
//   is a statement by a person to the operator of this deployment, and every
//   campaign leaves through the same authenticated mailbox and the same SPF /
//   DKIM records. A per-operator list would let operator B mail an address
//   that unsubscribed from operator A under a From: header the recipient
//   cannot tell apart -- one domain, one reputation, one complaint. So the
//   list is cluster-wide, and it is keyed by a SHA-256 digest of the
//   normalized address rather than by the address itself, so being
//   cluster-wide costs nobody an address: a caller can ask "is this address
//   suppressed" only about an address it already holds, and reading the whole
//   table yields digests and domains, never a mailbox.
//
// WHAT THE SENDING ENGINE IS: component/campaigns (the drain worker) plus
// integrations/campaigns (the DSL-callable surface). It sends DIRECTLY through
// the email integration rather than staging v1:platform:outboundRequest rows,
// which is a change from what this header said before memql#3348 -- see the
// `delivery.outboundRequestId` field doc for the three reasons.

use accounts.concepts.{ account }
use authoring.concepts.{ bundle }
use identity.concepts.{ user }
use platform.concepts.{ outboundRequest }

/// A named set of email recipients -- the WHO of a campaign. Audiences are authored in the portal
/// and referenced by campaigns; membership lives on v1:campaigns:recipient rows pointing back here
/// rather than as a list on this row, so a hundred-thousand-address audience is a hundred thousand
/// rows rather than one enormous payload. Per-row authz: owned -- ownerUserId is stamped from
/// actor.userId on every write and every read gates on ownerUserId==actor.userId.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="description", tertiary="ownerUserId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept audience {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this audience. Stamped from actor.userId at create time and re-stamped on every update, so ownership can never be reassigned by a caller. The load-bearing per-row authz key.")
  name         string!  @description("Short operator-facing name for the audience, e.g. 'Beta waitlist'. The primary display field; not required to be unique.")
  description  string   @description("What this audience is and how someone lands in it. Free text, shown under the name in the portal's audience list.")
  status       enum("active", "archived")  @default("active")  @description("Lifecycle. 'active' audiences are offered when authoring a campaign; 'archived' ones are kept for the history their past campaigns reference but are hidden from the picker. Audiences are never deleted -- a deleted audience would orphan every delivery record that names it.")
  accountId    string   @description("Optional v1:accounts:account.id this audience is FOR -- the client whose work it is. A RECORD, NEVER A VISIBILITY SCOPE (accounts D1): no query in this tree narrows a read because of it, and two people opening the same account see different rollups because the account is shared and the rows are not. Empty is the ordinary case and means the operator's own work; the OS app requires picking one at create, which is app behaviour rather than schema.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="forAccount", field="accountId", target=account, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One email address in an audience, with the subscription state that decides whether it may be
/// mailed. Subscription state is modelled here rather than derived at send time because it is a
/// standing fact about a person, not a property of one campaign: an unsubscribe must suppress every
/// future send, including sends of campaigns authored before the unsubscribe. Per-row authz: owned
/// via ownerUserId, mirrored from the parent audience at create time.
@displayCard(primary="email", secondary="displayName", tertiary="audienceId", status="subscriptionStatus")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept recipient {
  ownerUserId         string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this recipient row -- the owner of the parent audience, stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  audienceId          string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id this address belongs to. A person in two audiences is two rows, so a per-audience unsubscribe is expressible and a global one is a sweep.")
  email               string!  @pii  @description("The recipient's email address -- the delivery target and the identity of the row. Marked @pii: it is scrubbed by the deletion path and every query projecting it must constrain its caller.")
  displayName         string   @pii  @description("Optional human name used to personalize the greeting. Marked @pii for the same reason as email.")
  subscriptionStatus  enum("subscribed", "unsubscribed", "bounced", "complained")  @default("subscribed")  @description("Whether this address may be mailed. 'subscribed' is the only sendable state. 'unsubscribed' is the recipient's own choice and is permanent until they opt back in; 'bounced' and 'complained' are the provider's verdict and exist as distinct states because they mean different things to a deliverability review -- a bounce is a bad address, a complaint is a reputational event.")
  unsubscribedAt      datetime  @description("When the address left the subscribed state. Empty while subscribed. Recorded rather than inferred from row history so a suppression check is one field read.")
  source              enum("manual", "import", "signup")  @default("manual")  @description("How the address got here. 'manual' = typed into the portal, 'import' = bulk upload, 'signup' = a product flow added it. Provenance a deliverability review asks for first when complaint rates rise.")
  fields              object  @pii  @description("Per-recipient merge data -- every column a CSV import carried that was not `email` or `displayName`, verbatim, as a flat string map (memql#4822). Reachable from a template as {{fields.<key>}} against the CLOSED merge-tag set; the renderer builds a strings.NewReplacer over the enumerable keys rather than evaluating a template language, so a value here is substituted and never executed. Marked @pii: an import's spare columns routinely carry names, companies and account numbers, and the deletion path must reach them.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The authored content of a campaign -- subject line plus plain-text and optional HTML bodies. Kept
/// separate from the campaign so one piece of content can be sent to several audiences without being
/// retyped, and so editing copy is a different action (and a different authorization decision, later)
/// from scheduling a send. Per-row authz: owned.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="subject", tertiary="ownerUserId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept template {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this template. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  name         string!  @description("Operator-facing name for the template, e.g. 'August product update'. Distinct from the subject line, which is what the recipient sees.")
  subject      string!  @description("The email subject line. Rendered verbatim; the engine's email sender rejects control characters in it (header-injection barrier in integrations/email).")
  textBody     string!  @description("The plain-text body. Required, not optional: a text alternative is what keeps a message out of the spam bucket and readable in a client that refuses HTML.")
  htmlBody     string   @description("Optional HTML alternative. When present the message goes out as multipart/alternative with text first.")
  status       enum("draft", "ready", "archived")  @default("draft")  @description("Authoring lifecycle. 'draft' is being written; 'ready' means an operator has decided it may be attached to a campaign; 'archived' keeps it readable for the campaigns that referenced it without offering it in the picker.")
  accountId    string   @description("Optional v1:accounts:account.id this template is FOR -- the client whose work it is. A RECORD, NEVER A VISIBILITY SCOPE (accounts D1): no query in this tree narrows a read because of it, and two people opening the same account see different rollups because the account is shared and the rows are not. Empty is the ordinary case and means the operator's own work; the OS app requires picking one at create, which is app behaviour rather than schema.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="forAccount", field="accountId", target=account, direction="outgoing")
}

/// A mailbox this deployment may send campaign mail AS -- the "from whom" of a send (memql#4821,
/// program P1).
///
/// IT CARRIES NO SECRET MATERIAL, and that is the whole shape of the design. Authentication stays
/// the cluster's ONE Graph credential; an identity row is the operator's DECLARATION that a mailbox
/// exists inside their tenant and that campaigns may leave from it. Nothing here grants access to
/// anything. Whether the send actually succeeds is decided by the tenant's Exchange
/// ApplicationAccessPolicy -- the mail-enabled security group that scopes Mail.Send -- which no
/// engine preflight can read. So a mailbox declared here but not added to that group surfaces as
/// Graph's own 403 on the campaign's lastError, and that refusal is the honest check. The runbook
/// section "adding a sending identity" is the other half of creating one of these.
///
/// WHY A ROW RATHER THAN MORE ENV VARS. The deployment runs campaigns for several accounts, and
/// which mailbox a client's mail leaves from is a per-account fact an operator changes without a
/// restart. The env-configured sender remains the DEFAULT -- a campaign naming no identity behaves
/// exactly as every campaign did before this concept existed -- so plurality is purely additive and
/// the single-mailbox deployment never has to know this concept is here.
///
/// SUPPRESSION DOES NOT FOLLOW THE IDENTITY (program P8). Several mailboxes inside one operator
/// tenant are still one legal sender org and one domain reputation, so the do-not-mail list stays
/// cluster-wide: an unsubscribe is a statement to the operator of this deployment, not to one of
/// their mailboxes. Reputation and warmup DO key per identity -- they always did, on
/// v1:campaigns:reputationWindow.sendingIdentity and v1:campaigns:warmupState.sendingIdentity, which
/// were built for a plurality that did not yet exist. This concept is what finally supplies it.
///
/// Per-row authz: composite. The operator owns their identities; the cluster owner reads them all.
@displayCard(primary="address", secondary="fromName", tertiary="accountId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept senderIdentity {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this identity. Stamped from actor.userId on every write. The per-row authz key.")
  address      string!  @description("The mailbox UPN the Graph application may send as, e.g. news@acme-client.com. Becomes the From ADDRESS and the /users/{address}/sendMail path segment, so it is validated for RFC 5322 shape AND header safety on the write path -- a CR or LF here would be header injection into every message this identity sends. Normalized lowercase; it is also the reputation and warmup key, so two spellings of one mailbox would split its ramp in half.")
  fromName     string!  @description("The display name on the From header for campaigns sent as this identity, e.g. 'Acme News'. Overridden per campaign by campaign.fromName when that is set. Required, because a From with no phrase shows the raw mailbox to every recipient, which is the deliverability equivalent of not having thought about it.")
  replyTo      string   @description("Default Reply-To for campaigns using this identity that set none of their own. Empty means replies go to the sending address itself. A campaign's own replyTo always wins.")
  accountId    string   @description("Optional v1:accounts:account.id this identity belongs to -- the client whose mailbox it is. A RECORD, NEVER A VISIBILITY SCOPE (accounts D1); it is what lets the app PREFILL a campaign's identity picker from the selected account. The ENGINE never infers an identity from a campaign's accountId: prefill is UX, resolution is explicit (D4).")
  status       enum("active", "disabled")  @default("active")  @description("Whether this identity may be used. 'disabled' is how an operator retires a mailbox WITHOUT deleting the row -- past campaigns name it, and the reputation history is keyed on its address. Preflight refuses a campaign naming a disabled identity rather than falling back to the default, because a silent fallback mails a client's list from the wrong mailbox and nothing says so.")
  notes        string   @description("Free-text operator provenance -- which tenant group the mailbox is in, who asked for it, when it was warmed. Never a credential.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="forAccount", field="accountId", target=account, direction="outgoing")
}

/// A campaign: one template addressed to one audience, optionally at a scheduled time, plus the state
/// of its send. This is the row an operator thinks of as 'the campaign'. Per-row authz: owned.
///
/// The send-state fields (startedAt / completedAt / the three counters / lastError) are written by
/// the sending engine's drain worker through `updateCampaignProgress`, which runs under the
/// CAMPAIGN OWNER'S actor -- so `ownerUserId` is re-stamped from `actor.userId` there exactly as it
/// is on the operator-facing writes, and the counters land on a row the worker was authorized to
/// touch rather than on one it reached past the owner tier.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="scheduledAt", tertiary="audienceId", status="status")
// The Materializer's mark (epic memql#4977). "How did last quarter's sends
// go" is a document somebody writes by hand today. `fields` carries the
// campaign's identity and its outcome and NOT its counters: those live on
// the send job and the delivery ledger, and a compose prompt handed a
// frozen estimate would report it as a result.
@composable(as="campaign", fields="name,status,scheduledAt,startedAt,completedAt,lastError", list="campaigns")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept campaign {
  ownerUserId     string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this campaign. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  name            string!  @description("Operator-facing campaign name. The primary display field.")
  audienceId      string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id this campaign is addressed to. Resolved to recipient rows at send time, not at authoring time, so an audience that grows between scheduling and sending is mailed as it stands when the send runs.")
  templateId      string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies.")
  fromName        string   @description("Per-campaign display name on the From header, e.g. 'The MemQL team'. A non-secret presentation setting: it overrides the integration-wide default (MEMQL_EMAIL_FROM_NAME) for this campaign only. The From ADDRESS is deliberately not settable here -- it is bound to the configured mailbox credential and changing it is a deliverability decision, not a campaign one.")
  replyTo         string   @description("Optional Reply-To address for this campaign. Non-secret; empty means replies go to the configured sender address.")
  scheduledAt     datetime  @description("When the send should begin. Empty means the campaign is not scheduled -- it is a draft an operator will start by hand. THE AUTHORITY on when a scheduled send fires (memql#3459): the drain worker re-reads this field rather than trusting the copy on the send job, so moving the date here moves the send.")
  status          enum("draft", "scheduled", "sending", "paused", "sent", "cancelled", "failed")  @default("draft")  @description("Campaign lifecycle. 'draft' is being authored; 'scheduled' is committed to a time and WILL fire on its own (memql#3459 -- the worker promotes the campaign's scheduled send job once scheduledAt has passed); 'sending' / 'paused' / 'sent' / 'failed' are send-engine states; 'cancelled' is an operator stopping it before or during a send.")
  startedAt       datetime  @description("When the send actually began. Empty until it does. Distinct from scheduledAt: the gap between the two is the queue latency a deliverability review looks at.")
  completedAt     datetime  @description("When the last recipient reached a terminal delivery state.")
  recipientCount  int  @default("0")  @description("How many recipients the audience resolved to when the send started. Frozen at send time so the counters below add up even if the audience changes afterwards.")
  sentCount       int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries the transport accepted. Denormalized from v1:campaigns:delivery so a campaign list does not have to aggregate per row.")
  failedCount     int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries that reached a terminal failure.")
  lastError       string   @description("The most recent send-level error -- a refused configuration, an exhausted allowlist -- as opposed to a per-recipient failure, which lives on the delivery row. Empty on a clean send.")
  skippedCount    int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients suppressed rather than mailed (memql#4823). The worker has computed this per job since memql#3348 and had nowhere on the CAMPAIGN to put it, so the number an operator most needs -- the gap between the audience and what actually left -- was visible only on a clusterOwner-tier row a browser cannot read. recipientCount minus sentCount minus this minus failedCount is what is still outstanding.")
  accountId       string   @description("Optional v1:accounts:account.id this campaign is FOR -- the client whose work it is. A RECORD, NEVER A VISIBILITY SCOPE (accounts D1): no query in this tree narrows a read because of it. Empty is the ordinary case and means the operator's own work; the OS app requires picking one at create, which is app behaviour rather than schema.")
  senderIdentityId string  @description("Optional v1:campaigns:senderIdentity.id this campaign sends AS. Empty resolves to the env-configured default sender, which is exactly what every campaign did before identities existed -- so absence is the ordinary case and plurality is additive. THE ENGINE NEVER INFERS THIS FROM accountId: the app prefills the picker from the account's identities, and prefill is UX while resolution is explicit (memql#4821 D4). Preflight refuses a campaign naming a missing or disabled identity rather than silently falling back, because a silent fallback mails a client's list from the wrong mailbox.")
  trackOpens      bool  @default("true")  @description("Whether the HTML part carries a tracking pixel (memql#4823). HTML only -- there is no honest way to count an open in a plain-text part, and a text-part beacon would be a claim the format cannot support. Default true; an operator sending to a privacy-sensitive list turns it off per campaign.")
  trackClicks     bool  @default("true")  @description("Whether http(s) links in the HTML part are rewritten to signed redirect URLs (memql#4823). The signature is what makes the redirect open-redirect-proof: an unsigned or tampered target never redirects. Text-part link tracking is deliberately not built -- rewriting a URL a reader can see would be visible mangling for a number nobody asked for.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="templateId", target=template, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="sendsAs", field="senderIdentityId", target=senderIdentity, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="forAccount", field="accountId", target=account, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One recipient's outcome for one campaign -- the per-recipient audit trail a deliverability review
/// is actually made of, and the LEDGER THAT MAKES A RESUMED SEND SAFE. Per-row authz: owned.
///
/// IDEMPOTENCY IS STRUCTURAL, not a retry flag. The row id is derived deterministically from
/// (campaignId, recipientId) -- per-part hashed, so no separator can alias two pairs onto one id --
/// which means a recipient has exactly one delivery row per campaign no matter how many times the
/// send is resumed, restarted or raced between replicas. The worker's batch is "recipients of this
/// audience with no delivery row, plus rows still `pending` whose nextAttemptAt has passed", so a
/// recipient that already reached `sent` or `skipped` is simply not in the next batch. There is no
/// "already sent?" flag to forget to check: the absence of the row IS the work queue.
///
/// Written by component/campaigns' drain worker through `recordCampaignDelivery`, which runs under
/// the CAMPAIGN OWNER'S actor -- so `ownerUserId` is stamped from `actor.userId` like every other
/// write in this domain, never from a caller argument. That is what memql#3348 supplies and what
/// took v1:campaigns:delivery off the owner-stamping gate's exemption list.
///
/// outboundRequestId is EMPTY on every row this engine writes, and that is a decision rather than an
/// omission (memql#3348). memql#3323 designed the send to stage v1:platform:outboundRequest rows and
/// let the engine's outbound worker deliver them. Three things make that the wrong seam for bulk
/// campaign mail: (1) the campaign worker has to SEE the provider's 429 + Retry-After to pace
/// itself, and a queue drained by another worker turns that signal into an opaque retry -- which is
/// exactly the "retries that look like duplicates to the recipient" failure; (2) a per-recipient
/// TERMINAL outcome is the whole point of this row, and an async seam leaves every row `pending`
/// until somebody writes a reconciler; (3) MEMQL_OUTBOUND_EMAIL_ALLOWLIST is a domain allowlist for
/// TRANSACTIONAL mail, and a marketing audience is by definition arbitrary external domains -- so
/// routing campaigns through it would either refuse every send or force that allowlist wide open and
/// weaken the transactional egress control it exists to provide. The field stays on the schema
/// because a future queued transport would populate it.
@displayCard(primary="email", secondary="campaignId", tertiary="sentAt", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept delivery {
  ownerUserId        string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this delivery record -- the owner of the campaign, mirrored so the row is gated without a join. The per-row authz key.")
  campaignId         string!  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id this delivery belongs to.")
  recipientId        string!  @description("v1:campaigns:recipient.id that was targeted.")
  email              string!  @pii  @description("The address the send targeted, captured at send time rather than read back from the recipient row -- an address that changes afterwards must not rewrite what was actually mailed. Marked @pii.")
  status             enum("pending", "sent", "failed", "skipped")!  @description("Outcome for this recipient. 'sent' and 'skipped' and 'failed' are terminal; 'pending' means a retryable transport failure whose next attempt is due at nextAttemptAt (a row is never written as 'pending' before an attempt -- the ABSENCE of the row is the not-yet-attempted state, see the concept doc). 'skipped' is the suppression case -- unsubscribed, hard-bounced or complained -- and is distinct from 'failed' because it is a correct outcome rather than an error.")
  outboundRequestId  string   @description("v1:platform:outboundRequest.id carrying this message, when a queued transport staged one. EMPTY on every row the current engine writes -- it delivers directly so it can observe the provider's throttle response. See the concept doc for the three reasons.")
  skipReason         string   @description("Why a 'skipped' delivery was suppressed: 'unsubscribed' / 'hard_bounce' / 'complaint' / 'manual' when the cluster suppression list matched, or the recipient row's own subscriptionStatus when that was the gate. Empty otherwise.")
  lastError          string   @description("The transport's error for a 'failed' or retrying 'pending' delivery, truncated to 4KB. Empty otherwise.")
  sentAt             datetime  @description("When the transport accepted the message. Empty unless status is 'sent'.")
  attempts           int  @default("0")  @description("Delivery attempts made for this recipient. Incremented on each transport failure and bounded by MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_MAX_ATTEMPTS, past which the row goes terminal 'failed'. Zero on a 'skipped' row, which never reaches the transport.")
  emailRuleId        string   @description("v1:campaigns:emailRule.id of the event rule that produced this send, when one did (memql#4829). Empty on every ordinary campaign delivery, which is the overwhelming majority. It exists because an event rule mails one person at a time in response to something that happened, and 'which rule mailed this person, and why' is the FIRST question anybody asks about automated mail -- a question the campaign id alone cannot answer, because a rule's sends carry no campaign at all.")
  nextAttemptAt      datetime  @description("When a retryable 'pending' row becomes eligible again -- bounded exponential backoff with jitter, or the provider's own Retry-After when it supplied one. Compared Go-side by the drain worker, which is why it is a stored instant rather than a computed one.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="recipientId", target=recipient, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="outboundRequestId", target=outboundRequest, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="sentByRule", field="emailRuleId", target=emailRule, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One tracked interaction with one delivered message -- an open or a click (memql#4823). Append-only:
/// a person who opens a message four times produces four rows, and `campaignStats` computes the
/// unique figure by (delivery, kind). Raw events are stored rather than counters incremented because
/// a counter cannot answer "when", and "the whole audience opened it within ninety seconds of a
/// tweet" is the shape of question an operator actually has.
///
/// HOW A ROW GETS HERE, AND WHY THAT IS SAFE. The tracking endpoints are unauthenticated by
/// necessity -- the caller is a mail client fetching an image, or a browser following a link -- so
/// the only thing standing between them and a forged row is the token. The token is HMAC-signed by
/// the same rotating key ring the unsubscribe links use, under its OWN context label, and it carries
/// the delivery, the campaign, the kind and (for a click) the destination URL. An unsigned or edited
/// token records NOTHING and, for a click, redirects nowhere: the signature over the URL is exactly
/// what makes the redirect open-redirect-proof. The write itself is a @serverOnly mutation under
/// internal origin, so no client can reach it directly whatever it holds.
///
/// WHAT AN OPEN IS NOT. It is a pixel fetch, and mail clients cache, prefetch and block images. An
/// open is therefore evidence of interest and never proof of reading, and the surfaces that render
/// it say so rather than implying a precision the mechanism does not have. Text-only readers count
/// as zero opens, which is the same statement.
///
/// Per-row authz: composite -- the operator owns their own engagement history, the cluster owner
/// reads every operator's.
@displayCard(primary="kind", secondary="campaignId", tertiary="occurredAt", status="kind")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept engagementEvent {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns the campaign this event is about, mirrored so the row is gated without a join. The per-row authz key. Taken from the signed token's own campaign, never from the request.")
  campaignId   string!  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id the tracked message belonged to. Denormalized from the delivery so per-campaign stats need no join.")
  deliveryId   string!  @description("v1:campaigns:delivery.id -- the one recipient's message that was opened or clicked. It is the uniqueness key: unique opens are distinct deliveryIds, not distinct addresses, because one address in two audiences is two people as far as a campaign is concerned.")
  kind         enum("open", "click")!  @description("Which interaction. 'open' is a tracking-pixel fetch on the HTML part; 'click' is a signed redirect being followed. There is deliberately no third value -- 'delivered' is not observable here (the transport says accepted and a bounce arrives later) and inventing it would be a number nothing can honestly produce.")
  url          string   @description("For a click, the destination the reader was sent to, verbatim from the signed token. Empty on an open. Stored so 'which link worked' is answerable without re-parsing the body a template may since have been edited out from under.")
  occurredAt   datetime!  @description("When the hit arrived, stamped from the engine clock. Not caller-supplied: the caller here is an unauthenticated mail client, and a timestamp it chose would be the one field of this row an attacker could set.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="deliveryId", target=delivery, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The engine's own record of one send RUN -- the queue the drain worker scans, and the only row in
/// this domain the worker can find without already knowing whose it is.
///
/// WHY THIS IS clusterOwner AND NOT owned. A worker cannot scan an owned concept: the owned tier
/// injects `ownerUserId==actor.userId` into every read and there is no cluster-owner bypass on it
/// (component/memql/rowauthz_enforce.go), so "which campaigns want sending" is not a question an
/// owned row can answer. Nor should it be answerable from a row a caller can write: whichever row
/// says "send campaign X as user Y" is an instruction the engine carries out under Y's authority, so
/// a caller-writable one is an escalation with extra steps. Both writers of this concept
/// (campaignStartSend and the worker) are Go that derived campaignOwnerUserId from a campaign row it
/// had ALREADY READ under the caller's own actor -- the caller proves ownership by being able to
/// read their own campaign, and nothing they typed is trusted.
///
/// One row per campaign, id = the campaign's bare short id, so pause / resume / restart all land on
/// the same timeline rather than accumulating runs. Per-recipient idempotency does not live here --
/// it lives on the delivery ledger (see v1:campaigns:delivery), which is what makes a resumed send
/// safe even if this row is lost entirely.
///
/// A SCHEDULED CAMPAIGN IS A JOB IN THE `scheduled` STATUS (memql#3459), and that is why scheduling
/// needed no new concept and no cross-owner scan. "Which campaigns are due?" is not a question an
/// OWNED row can answer -- the owned tier injects ownerUserId==actor.userId into every read with no
/// cluster-owner bypass -- so a clock-driven scan over v1:campaigns:campaign is not expressible at
/// all. The scheduling builtin therefore does what campaignStartSend already does: it reads the
/// campaign under the CALLER'S own actor, and writes a job here carrying the owner it derived. The
/// worker then scans this concept, which it can, and asks the campaign row whether its time has come.
///
/// `scheduled` is deliberately NOT drainable (see isDrainableSendJob): a job in this status is
/// inert until something promotes it to `queued`, so the row existing can never by itself mail
/// anybody.
@displayCard(primary="campaignId", secondary="status", tertiary="startedAt", status="status")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept sendJob {
  campaignId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id this job drains. Stored canonical like every other @relationship field; the worker bare-ifies it (memql.BareShortId) before feeding it back to campaignById, because a named-query argument is the BARE-ids client contract.")
  campaignOwnerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id the worker impersonates for every owned-tier read and write this job performs -- the campaign, its template, its audience's recipients, and the delivery ledger. SERVER-DERIVED ONLY: both writers read it off a campaign row they had already read under the caller's own actor, so it can never name a user the caller could not already act as. This is the single most security-load-bearing field in the domain.")
  audienceId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id resolved to recipients each batch, so an audience that grows mid-send is mailed as it stands when the batch runs.")
  templateId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies.")
  status               enum("scheduled", "queued", "running", "paused", "completed", "failed", "cancelled")!  @description("Job lifecycle. 'scheduled' is committed to a time and NOT drainable -- the worker promotes it to 'queued' once the campaign row's scheduledAt has passed, so a scheduled job can never mail anybody by merely existing; 'queued' is enqueued and unclaimed; 'running' has had at least one batch; 'paused' is an operator stopping the drain without discarding the ledger; 'completed' means every recipient reached a terminal delivery state; 'failed' is a job-level refusal (no configured sender, no unsubscribe secret, audience over the ceiling); 'cancelled' follows the campaign being cancelled.")
  scheduledAt          datetime  @description("The time this job was enqueued for, copied off the campaign at schedule time. A HINT for the operator and for the scan, never the authority: the worker re-reads the CAMPAIGN's scheduledAt at fire time, so an operator who moves the date with updateCampaign is obeyed rather than raced by a stale copy. Empty on a job started by hand.")
  recipientCount       int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients the audience resolved to on the most recent batch. Not frozen -- the campaign row carries the frozen count.")
  sentCount            int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries the transport accepted so far.")
  skippedCount         int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients suppressed rather than mailed. The gap between this and sentCount IS the suppression rate a deliverability review asks for.")
  failedCount          int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients that reached a terminal failure.")
  lastError            string  @description("The most recent job-level error, truncated. Empty on a clean run.")
  startedAt            datetime  @description("When the first batch ran.")
  completedAt          datetime  @description("When the job reached a terminal status.")
  rosterCursor         string  @description("Where the roster walk resumes (memql#3460). An OPAQUE engine keyset cursor -- the (createdAt, id) position of the last recipient examined, encoded by the engine's own pagination codec -- so a send through a large audience does not re-scan from the start on every 15-second tick. Empty means 'start a fresh pass'. It is an OPTIMIZATION, never a correctness mechanism: the delivery ledger decides who gets mailed, so a cursor lost, stale or refused by a codec change costs one re-scan and nothing else.")
  rosterOutstanding    bool  @description("Whether the pass currently in progress has seen ANY recipient still to deal with -- unmailed, or pending a retry that is not yet due (memql#3460). It is what makes completion decidable without holding the whole roster in memory: a pass that reaches the end of the audience with this false has proved there is nothing left, and the job completes. Reset at the start of each pass.")
  throttledUntil       datetime  @description("When the provider's own throttle response says we may resume. Set from a 429/503 Retry-After; the worker leaves the job alone until it passes. Distinct from our own pacing, which is a per-process token bucket and needs no row.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="campaignOwnerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="templateId", target=template, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The cluster-wide do-not-mail list. ONE address per row, keyed by the SHA-256 hex digest of the
/// normalized address (lowercased, trimmed) -- the digest IS the row id, so a lookup is a by-id read
/// and a re-suppression is a new version of the same row rather than a duplicate.
///
/// SUPPRESSION IS CLUSTER-WIDE, NOT PER-ACCOUNT, and that is the decision this concept exists to
/// record (memql#3348). An unsubscribe is a statement by a person to the operator of THIS
/// DEPLOYMENT: every campaign leaves through the same authenticated mailbox, under the same From
/// address, signed by the same SPF and DKIM records. A per-operator list would let operator B mail
/// an address that unsubscribed from operator A, in a message the recipient cannot tell apart from
/// the one they already opted out of -- and the complaint lands on the one shared domain reputation.
/// Legally the obligation attaches to the sender, and there is one sender.
///
/// BEING CLUSTER-WIDE COSTS NOBODY AN ADDRESS. The row stores no mailbox: the id is a non-reversible
/// digest and the only human-readable field is the address's DOMAIN, which is what a deliverability
/// review actually asks for ("which domains are bouncing?") and which identifies no one. A caller
/// can therefore ask "is this address suppressed" only about an address it already holds.
///
/// Reads and writes are clusterOwner because the list must not be caller-writable: a caller who
/// could insert digests could silently suppress a competitor's audience, and one who could delete
/// them could un-suppress a complaint. Every write goes through the engine -- the unsubscribe
/// endpoint, the feedback builtin, or an operator's explicit suppress call -- and each of those
/// derives the address from something it verified rather than from an argument. An operator's own
/// view of who left is their recipient rows' subscriptionStatus, which the worker converges onto
/// this list on the next send.
@displayCard(primary="domain", secondary="reason", tertiary="suppressedAt", status="reason")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept suppression {
  reason            enum("unsubscribed", "hard_bounce", "complaint", "manual")!  @description("Why this address may never be mailed again. 'unsubscribed' is the recipient's own choice, via the RFC 8058 one-click header or the link in the body. 'hard_bounce' and 'complaint' are the provider's verdict and are kept apart because they mean different things to a review -- a bounce is a dead address, a complaint is a reputational event. 'manual' is an operator adding one by hand.")
  domain            string  @description("The domain part of the suppressed address, lowercased. Recorded rather than derived because the address itself is never stored: it is what makes 'which domains are bouncing' answerable without making 'who unsubscribed' answerable.")
  suppressedAt      datetime!  @description("When the address entered the list. Stamped from the engine clock at write time, not threaded by a caller -- unlike a recipient row's unsubscribedAt, which can legitimately record a state change that happened earlier.")
  sourceCampaignId  string  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id of the send that produced this suppression, when one did. Empty for an operator-entered row or a feedback report that named no campaign.")
  note              string  @description("Free-text provenance for a 'manual' row, or the provider's bounce classification for a reported one. Never the address.")

  @relationship(type="references", field="sourceCampaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
}

/// Per-(sending identity, recipient domain, day) counters -- the reputation telemetry a warming ramp
/// has to be driven by, and the thing memql#3462 says has to exist before a ramp is anything but a
/// hardcoded table pretending to be a control loop.
///
/// WHY PER DOMAIN. Mailbox providers judge a sender independently. "Our bounce rate is 1%" is not a
/// number any of them acts on; "our complaint rate at gmail.com is 0.4% this week" is. It is also the
/// question an operator asks long before they want anything automated, which is why this half is
/// worth having on its own.
///
/// WHY A ROW PER REPLICA (nodeId). Counters are accumulated in each worker's memory and written as
/// ABSOLUTE values to that replica's own row. Two replicas incrementing one shared row would need a
/// read-modify-write on the send path and would lose counts to the interleaving; one row each, summed
/// at read time, has neither problem. The cost is that a reader must aggregate, which it was going to
/// do anyway across domains and days.
///
/// WHAT `accepted` IS NOT. It counts messages the TRANSPORT accepted, which is not delivery -- Graph
/// answers 202 and the bounce arrives later. Delivery is therefore inferred (accepted minus bounces)
/// and the field is named for what it actually observes. A `delivered` column would be a number
/// nothing on this side can honestly produce.
///
/// UNSUBSCRIBES ARE NOT COUNTED HERE, deliberately: the one-click endpoint is a separate HTTP handler
/// with its own store and no collector, so a field for them would be a field nothing writes -- the
/// exact shape this issue exists to remove. The ramp's thresholds are the bounce and complaint rates,
/// which are what the providers themselves act on.
///
/// clusterOwner tier, for the same reason the suppression list is: one deployment, one sending
/// mailbox, one reputation. It is not an operator's number and no operator's authority should decide
/// what it says. It carries no address -- a domain and four integers.
@displayCard(primary="domain", secondary="windowStart", tertiary="sendingIdentity", status="sendingIdentity")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept reputationWindow {
  sendingIdentity  string!  @description("Which sending mailbox these counters belong to. One deployment normally has exactly one, and the default is derived from the configured sender address; the dimension exists so a future multi-mailbox deployment is a value change rather than a re-model.")
  domain           string!  @description("The recipient's domain, lowercased -- gmail.com, outlook.com. The only human-readable field, and it identifies nobody, which is the same property that makes the suppression list safe to keep cluster-wide.")
  windowStart      string!  @description("The UTC day these counters cover, as YYYY-MM-DD. A string rather than a datetime because it is a BUCKET KEY that has to be compared for equality and ordered lexically, and because it participates in the derived row id.")
  nodeId           string!  @description("The replica that accumulated these counts. Part of the row id; see the concept doc for why one row per replica beats one shared row.")
  accepted         int  @default("0")  @description("Messages the transport accepted for this domain in this window. NOT deliveries -- Graph answers 202 and a bounce arrives later. Delivery is inferred as accepted minus bounces.")
  hardBounce       int  @default("0")  @description("Permanent bounces reported for this domain, from the provider's own classification (memql#3461). The numerator of the rate a ramp holds on.")
  softBounce       int  @default("0")  @description("Transient bounces. Watched but not thresholded: a full mailbox is not a reputational event.")
  complaint        int  @default("0")  @description("Spam complaints reported for this domain. The most damaging signal a mailbox provider tracks, and the one whose threshold is an order of magnitude tighter than the bounce rate's.")
}

/// The warming ramp's own state -- one row per sending identity, id = the sending identity
/// (memql#3462).
///
/// A RAMP THAT ADVANCES ON A CLOCK IS A GUESS WITH A SCHEDULE ATTACHED, and the issue is explicit
/// that the reason no ramp shipped with memql#3348 was the absence of the evidence one would need.
/// So this row records a decision rather than a countdown: a step advances only when the previous
/// step produced enough volume, held long enough, and stayed inside the bounce and complaint
/// thresholds. `decision` and `reason` are what the row is FOR -- an operator who cannot see why the
/// ramp last held has a control loop they have to trust rather than read.
///
/// clusterOwner, like the counters it reads: the ramp paces the deployment's one sending mailbox.
@displayCard(primary="sendingIdentity", secondary="ratePerMinute", tertiary="evaluatedAt", status="decision")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept warmupState {
  sendingIdentity  string!  @description("The sending mailbox this ramp paces. Also the row id, so there is exactly one ramp per identity and no way to accumulate competing ones.")
  step             int  @default("0")  @description("Index into MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_WARMUP_STEPS. Zero is the starting trickle a new sending domain begins on.")
  ratePerMinute    int  @default("0")  @description("The send rate the current step allows. The worker takes the LOWER of this and MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_SEND_RATE_PER_MINUTE, so the ramp can only ever slow the configured rate down, never raise it past what the operator allowed.")
  decision         enum("advanced", "held", "reduced", "started")  @description("What the last evaluation did. 'held' is the common and healthy state -- a step waits for volume and time before it may advance.")
  reason           string  @description("Why, in words, stated for a person: which threshold was exceeded, or how much volume the step still needs. An automated pacer whose decisions cannot be read is one an operator has to trust rather than check.")
  evaluatedAt      datetime  @description("When the ramp last looked at the evidence.")
  stepEnteredAt    datetime  @description("When the current step began. The minimum-hold clock runs from here.")
  acceptedInStep   int  @default("0")  @description("Messages accepted since the current step began -- the volume half of the advance condition. A step that has sent nothing has produced no evidence, and advancing on no evidence is what this design exists to avoid.")
}

/// Append-only consent / suppression event for one subscriber (memql#4141).
/// Current status is derived from the latest event -- there is no mutable
/// status column on this concept. Send-time suppression (v1:campaigns:suppression
/// + recipient.subscriptionStatus) is unchanged; this stream is the
/// export/compliance answer for "status, date, source".
///
/// grant / withdraw / bounce / complaint / suppress are events. reason is
/// required on suppress (enforced by recordConsentSuppress). Config and
/// templates stay data, not this schema.
@displayCard(primary="kind", secondary="emailDigest", tertiary="occurredAt", status="kind")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept consentEvent {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id of the operator whose send this consent belongs to. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key for export.")
  emailDigest  string!  @description("SHA-256 hex digest of the normalized (lowercased, trimmed) address. The subscriber key. The mailbox is never stored here -- a caller can ask about an address only when it already holds it.")
  kind         enum("grant", "withdraw", "bounce", "complaint", "suppress")!  @description("The event. grant = opted in; withdraw = opted out; bounce / complaint = provider verdict; suppress = operator or policy suppression. There is no mutable status field -- current status is the latest kind.")
  source       enum("signup", "one_click", "import", "provider", "operator", "rfc8058")!  @description("Where the event came from. Data, not DSL source.")
  occurredAt   datetime!  @description("When the event happened. Stamped from the engine clock unless the writer records an earlier verified event (e.g. a provider timestamp).")
  reason       string   @description("Why, when kind is suppress. Required on the suppress write path. Empty for grant/withdraw/bounce/complaint.")
  recipientId  string   @description("Optional v1:campaigns:recipient.id when the event is about a specific audience membership. The digest is the subscriber identity; this is the membership the operator was looking at.")
  campaignId   string   @description("Optional v1:campaigns:campaign.id of the send that produced a bounce, complaint, or one-click withdraw.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="recipientId", target=recipient, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
}

/// An event-triggered email: "when X happens on concept Y, send template Z" (memql#4829, program P4
/// and P5). THIS ROW IS THE FORM, NOT THE MECHANISM. What actually runs is a real authored
/// automation construct, generated deterministically from these fields and activated through the
/// runtime authoring pipeline; this row is what the app lists, edits, pauses and explains.
///
/// WHY A GENERATED CONSTRUCT RATHER THAN ONE SHIPPED AUTOMATION WITH A LOOKUP TABLE. An automation's
/// @trigger names ONE concept at load time. A user picking an arbitrary trigger concept therefore
/// cannot be served by anything pre-shipped -- there is no wildcard trigger, and there should not be
/// one. Generating a construct also inherits the whole governance apparatus for free: per-rule pause,
/// the circuit breaker, the cluster kill switch, boot re-arm, and an author-scoped actor. The
/// generator is a deterministic template. The LLM authoring path stays off: a rule that mails
/// strangers is not a place for a model to improvise, and a deterministic generator is one a person
/// can read the output of.
///
/// TWO DELIVERY LANES, CHOSEN BY WHO RECEIVES (P5), never by a toggle the operator has to understand.
///
///   recipientMode=cluster_roles  OPERATIONAL. The recipients are this cluster's own people, so the
///                                mail rides the transactional outbox: the egress allowlist applies,
///                                there is no unsubscribe footer, and the marketing suppression list
///                                is neither consulted nor written. Telling an owner a new admin was
///                                added is not marketing, and running it through the campaign
///                                machinery would let an unsubscribe from a newsletter silence an
///                                operational alert.
///
///   recipientMode=audience       MARKETING. A campaign audience is being mailed one row at a time,
///   recipientMode=row_address    so it rides the campaign machinery: suppression checked at the
///                                point of send, unsubscribe attached, sender identity applied,
///                                outcome ledgered. row_address is the same lane with the address
///                                read off the triggering row instead of a roster.
///
/// Per-row authz: composite -- the operator owns their rules, the cluster owner reads them all. The
/// GENERATED construct runs under the author's own envelope, which is why the rule records who
/// authored it: an automation's actor is not the row owner's, and a caller-scoped read from inside
/// one returns nothing while looking entirely correct.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="triggerConcept", tertiary="templateId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId", clusterOwner)
concept emailRule {
  ownerUserId       string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that authored this rule. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key, AND the envelope the generated construct runs under -- see the concept doc's note on the actor trap.")
  name              string!  @description("Operator-facing rule name, e.g. 'Tell the owner about new admins'. The primary display field, and the basis of the generated construct's name, so it is what an operator sees in both places.")
  description       string   @description("What this rule is for, in the operator's own words. Rendered under the name; also carried onto the generated construct so somebody reading the DSL knows why it exists.")
  triggerConcept    string!  @description("The canonical concept id whose rows fire this rule, e.g. 'v1:identity:user'. Offered from the LIVE concepts registry rather than a hardcoded list, so a rule can name a concept a product bundle added after this release. It becomes the generated @trigger's concept argument verbatim.")
  eventKind         enum("created", "updated")!  @description("Which row event fires the rule. 'created' maps to node.created, 'updated' to node.updated. Deliberately only two: a delete has no row to read recipients or merge data from, so a rule triggered by one could describe nothing about what was deleted.")
  condition         string   @description("Optional boolean expression over the triggering event, evaluated by the automation's own condition step -- e.g. role==\"admin\". Empty fires on every event of the kind. It is the runtime STRING evaluator's grammar, which is the one surface where ! is legal; the generator validates it before the bundle is written rather than letting a typo become a construct that fails to load.")
  templateId        string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies. The same templates campaigns use, so copy is authored once -- and merge tags resolve against whichever lane runs, with {{fields.*}} empty on the operational lane, which has no recipient row.")
  recipientMode     enum("cluster_roles", "audience", "row_address")!  @description("WHO receives, which is also what picks the delivery lane (P5). 'cluster_roles' = this cluster's own people, operational lane. 'audience' = a campaign audience, marketing lane. 'row_address' = an address read off the triggering row, marketing lane. The app presents this as a question about recipients and never as a lane toggle: the lane is a consequence.")
  recipientRoles    []string  @description("For recipientMode=cluster_roles, which roles receive -- owner / admin / developer / writer / reader. Empty means the cluster owner alone, which is the common 'tell me when this happens' case.")
  audienceId        string   @description("The audience this rule mails, REQUIRED in both marketing modes and ignored on the operational lane. For recipientMode=audience it is the roster. For recipientMode=row_address it is the list the row-derived address is ENROLLED INTO, and that is not bookkeeping: an unsubscribe token is minted from (owner, recipient, campaign), so an address with no recipient row has no way to opt out -- and mailing marketing to somebody who cannot unsubscribe breaches the exact RFC 8058 stance the rest of this engine is built on. The address gets a real membership with a real subscription state, and the second send to it consults the state the first one gave it.")
  recipientField    string   @description("For recipientMode=row_address, the dotted path into the triggering row's payload that holds the address, e.g. 'primaryContactEmail'. Validated to resolve on the trigger concept at generate time, because a path that resolves to nothing is a rule that fires forever and mails nobody.")
  accountId         string   @description("Optional v1:accounts:account.id this rule is FOR. A record, never a visibility scope (accounts D1).")
  senderIdentityId  string   @description("Optional v1:campaigns:senderIdentity.id the marketing lane sends as. Empty resolves to the env-configured default, exactly as a campaign's does. Ignored on the operational lane, which leaves through the transactional outbox and its own configured sender.")
  status            enum("draft", "active", "paused", "failed")  @default("draft")  @description("Rule lifecycle. 'draft' has never been generated. 'active' has a live construct. 'paused' keeps the construct but disarms it -- the operator's own stop button. 'failed' means generation or validation refused, and lastError says what the engine said; the app renders that sentence rather than inventing one.")
  bundleId          string   @description("v1:authoring:bundle.id of the generated bundle. Empty until the rule is first activated. Editing a rule generates a NEW bundle and retires the old one, so this always names the bundle currently armed.")
  constructName     string   @description("The generated automation construct's name, derived deterministically from the rule id so a regeneration replaces rather than accumulates. What to grep for when somebody asks which automation sent an email.")
  lastError         string   @description("Why generation, validation or activation last refused -- the engine's own sentence, so the app can render it verbatim instead of paraphrasing a failure it does not understand. Empty on a healthy rule.")
  lastFiredAt       datetime  @description("When the generated construct last ran. A LIVENESS field: it moves on its own, so a surface that fingerprints it for arrival cues turns the rules list into a strobe. Display it; do not ring on it.")
  firedCount        int  @default("0")  @description("How many times the rule has fired since it was first activated. Liveness like lastFiredAt -- shown, never fingerprinted.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="templateId", target=template, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="sendsAs", field="senderIdentityId", target=senderIdentity, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="forAccount", field="accountId", target=account, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", as="generatedInto", field="bundleId", target=bundle, direction="outgoing")
}
